WEBVTT

00:00:02.010 --> 00:00:05.270
We start out our EVA at the Quest airlock.

00:00:05.270 --> 00:00:07.060
Jeff will be EV1.

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:10.140
He'll be coming out wearing the red stripes,

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:13.580
and Kate will hand out an ORU bag to him

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:15.320
that contains all the IDA tools.

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:19.120
She'll egress, and then they'll begin their translation forward

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:22.480
out to the very forward end of the space station

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:23.710
to do their work.

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:26.210
Jeff will translate around the starboard side

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:30.000
of the external stowage platform number two.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.860
there just a moment while Kate finishes her initial translation adaptation and then translates

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:40.700
over the top of the stowage platform out to pick up her foot restraint, which is on the

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:47.370
very forward edge of the ESP-2. Once she's got that, Jeff will translate forward on the

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:54.290
lab and that up over the top of the Node-2 and finally onto the PMA-2 that's at the

00:00:54.290 --> 00:01:00.000
forward end of the space station. He'll position the ORU bag

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:05.710
on a couple of handrails that are right on the zenith side of the PMA, and then open

00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:11.330
the bag so that they've got access to their tools and equipment.

00:01:11.330 --> 00:01:13.910
In the meantime, Kate begins her translation.

00:01:13.910 --> 00:01:19.690
She's going to translate up over the top of the lab, and then forward onto the node 2,

00:01:19.690 --> 00:01:26.680
and finally forward onto the PMA 2, where she translates down toward the nadir side

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:30.000
and locates the socket where her foot restraint will come.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.430
or worksite interface.

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:34.430
Once she has her foot restraint set up there,

00:01:34.430 --> 00:01:39.070
she'll be able to access the cables that were prepositioned

00:01:39.070 --> 00:01:41.570
by Butch Wilmore on a prior EVA.

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:45.670
They're tied off in a bundle to a couple of handrails

00:01:45.670 --> 00:01:48.720
that are located on the PMA.

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:51.220
While she's getting set up, Jeff will also

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:53.520
get his foot restraint and get it set up.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:57.330
It's currently located on the top of the Columbus module.

00:01:57.330 --> 00:02:00.000
He'll retrieve it from there and then move it down to

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:09.590
his worksite interface on the PMA, which is more zenith and a little bit toward port.

00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:15.350
From there, each of them have access to be able to reach their cables.

00:02:15.350 --> 00:02:19.070
Jeff's cables have also been pre-positioned on a prior EVA.

00:02:19.070 --> 00:02:23.620
Terry Burtz left two bundles of cables there for him to access.

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:30.000
Once they get into their foot restraints, they can attach their adjustable equipment tethers loosely to

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:31.950
the IDA.

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:36.670
Kate has one adjustable tether and in order to be able to reach between the handrail and

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:42.020
the IDA-2, Jeff actually has two tethers in series.

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:45.760
When they're in position, they'll give the go to the robotics operator on the ground

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:50.740
to release the IDA-2 and back the Dexter arm away.

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:54.700
At this point, the crew has complete control of the IDA.

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:59.730
They'll cinch the tethers down into place and then they'll begin mating the cables.

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:04.020
This is Kate's cable configuration on the nadir side.

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:07.160
Here we see her at the neutral buoyancy laboratory

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:09.550
practicing those cable mates.

00:03:09.550 --> 00:03:12.450
And while she's mating her cables on the nadir side,

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:15.300
Jeff is mating his on the zenith side.

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:18.320
He has three cables to initially mate.

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:21.440
These cables are providing heater power

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:26.300
to the docking adapter as well as the hook drive power

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:28.290
and the sensor power.

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:30.000
The switches will be connected

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:34.120
commanded by the internal crew member, IV crew member.

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:36.660
Toc will work at the IDA control panel,

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:40.730
which is in the Node 2 pressurized volume.

00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:43.430
As he operates the switches, it commands

00:03:43.430 --> 00:03:46.370
the hooks that are on the docking adapter

00:03:46.370 --> 00:03:49.110
to extend out, rotate around, and engage

00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:53.350
the passive hooks that are on the pressurized mated adapter.

00:03:53.350 --> 00:03:54.650
There's two sets of hooks.

00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:56.660
And while they drive the first set of hooks,

00:03:56.660 --> 00:03:58.540
the crew needs to remain still.

00:03:58.540 --> 00:04:00.000
But during the second drive, it's

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:05.900
stable enough that they can begin work installing a cover on the hemireflector that's on the

00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:09.110
nadir part of the PMA2.

00:04:09.110 --> 00:04:11.330
Then they're into their final cable mates.

00:04:11.330 --> 00:04:17.010
These final cable configurations remove the power from the hooks that we're driving to

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:22.950
engage the PMA and provide visiting vehicle hook power.

00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:26.090
Here you see Jeff working in the neutral buoyancy laboratory.

00:04:26.090 --> 00:04:30.000
He's removing a couple of jumpers that are required to do his

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:36.470
final cable mating. Once the cables are mated, they have some outfitting to do. First, they'll

00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:43.790
be covering up the H fixture that the Dexter robot arm was holding on to. Then there is another

00:04:43.790 --> 00:04:50.990
chemireflector that is on the PMA. They'll install a cover over the top of it and then move the

00:04:50.990 --> 00:04:58.530
tether that was holding that cover in place up to tether to the large protective cover that's

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:00.000
covering the IDA

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:06.900
that was in place for launch in the SpaceX Dragon there are several straps

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:11.340
that they need to work together to remove it's very similar to the PMA

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:15.440
covers that we've been installing and removing in the past here you see Kate

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:20.600
working in the NBL to remove the straps from the handrails holding it in place

00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:26.500
the two of them need to work together to stuff that cover into the ORU bag at

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:30.000
that point they can install the two hemi

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:35.490
reflectors onto the docking adapter and then the final thing that they need to

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:41.350
do is mate up the visiting vehicle power. It's one final cable configuration that

00:05:41.350 --> 00:05:47.200
needs to be made. Jeff will complete that mating and at this point that

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:53.370
docking adapter is ready to receive our new crew vehicles. The crew can pack up

00:05:53.370 --> 00:06:00.000
and clean up this work site. Kate will pick up her foot restraint and return it to the ESP2 from Wednesday

00:06:00.030 --> 00:06:07.200
game. Jeff has to pack up the ORU bag and also return his foot restraint. His

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:12.200
foot restraint goes back from where it's located, where he's been using it on the

00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:17.600
PMA. He'll take it back up to the top of the Columbus module. It'll be staged

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:23.180
there in place for the IDA 3 installation that will be happening in

00:06:23.180 --> 00:06:30.000
late 2017. They return the ORU bag back to the airlock and then go out, Kate

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:37.100
will go out to the lab nadir to a bag that's already there and route the cable for IDA3.

00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:41.420
We call it the white-green cable because we put some tape marks on it in white and green

00:06:41.420 --> 00:06:43.160
to indicate which one it is.

00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:49.810
She routes it forward to the node 2 where it's in position for the IDA3 installation.

00:06:49.810 --> 00:06:57.280
Jeff has some work to do back in the area where the Z1, the node 1, the node 3, and

00:06:57.280 --> 00:06:59.080
the lab all come together.

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:00.000
We refer to this area.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:05.080
is the rat's nest because of all of the cables and electrical lines and fluid lines that

00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:06.460
route through that area.

00:07:06.460 --> 00:07:13.500
He'll pick up the cable bundle that was left in position at the end of EVA 35, he'll make

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:21.120
a couple of connections down on the node 2 and then route that cable across the Z1 tray

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:26.340
over to the starboard side of that tray and then leave it in position there where it will

00:07:26.340 --> 00:07:29.880
be ready for its final routing on a future EVA.

00:07:30.220 --> 00:07:36.390
The last scheduled task for this spacewalk will be to remove a couple of caps from some

00:07:36.390 --> 00:07:43.720
connectors that are located just underneath the Z1 on the outside of the node one.

00:07:43.720 --> 00:07:47.630
At that point, the crew will return to the airlock and ingress.

00:07:47.630 --> 00:07:53.110
If we've been particularly lucky and efficient throughout the day and the hardware cooperates,

00:07:53.110 --> 00:07:56.770
rather than ingressing the airlock, we've got a couple of get-ahead tasks that we can

00:07:56.770 --> 00:07:57.760
do.

00:07:57.760 --> 00:08:00.000
One of them is to configure the port.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:04.870
CETA cart. We'll tie down the brake handles to keep them out of the way for

00:08:04.870 --> 00:08:09.950
long-term operations on station. And then we also want to get some photos of the

00:08:09.950 --> 00:08:14.610
Alpha Magnetic Spectrometer. From the windows and from all the cameras on

00:08:14.610 --> 00:08:19.530
Space Station we cannot see the outboard side of the AMS. We'd like to get some

00:08:19.530 --> 00:08:24.540
photos of that area, see how it's doing, so they can get those photos from the

00:08:24.540 --> 00:08:29.000
top of the logistics carrier that's located right next to it.
